artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider time-dependent density functional theory (TDDFT) applied to the single-band Hubbard model on a lattice with on-site interaction strength U > 0 and site density n ∈ [0,2]. Within the adiabatic local density approximation (ALDA), the exchange-correlation (xc) potential v_xc(n;U) is taken from the ground-state energy per site of the homogeneous Hubbard model (e.g., from the exact 1D Bethe-ansatz solution or from dynamical mean-field theory in higher dimensions), and the corresponding ALDA xc kernel is defined site-locally and time-locally as

   f^{ALDA}_{xc}(n;U) = ∂ v_xc(n;U) / ∂ n.

This kernel is known to display two qualitative features absent from standard continuum xc kernels:
  (i) sign change: f^{ALDA}_{xc}(n;U) can be either positive or negative depending on (n,U), so it can either reinforce or oppose the bare interaction U in the density-density response;
  (ii) singularity at half filling: for U exceeding the Mott-Hubbard critical value U^{Mott}_c (and for all U > 0 in one dimension), v_xc(n;U) is discontinuous at n = 1, producing a Dirac-delta-like spike in f^{ALDA}_{xc} at n = 1.

Problem. Develop a rigorous TDDFT exchange-correlation kernel f_{xc}(n,n';t-t';U) for the Hubbard model that:
  (a) reduces to f^{ALDA}_{xc}(n;U) in the local adiabatic limit;
  (b) incorporates spatial nonlocality (dependence on sites i ≠ j) and nonadiabatic memory (frequency dependence) in a controlled way;
  (c) preserves the sign-changing behavior in (i) and the singular structure at half filling associated with the Mott-Hubbard metal-insulator transition in (ii) when these features are physically present;
  (d) yields a linear density response function χ(q,ω) consistent with these qualitative features beyond the local adiabatic approximation.

In particular, characterize how the sign-change locus in the (n,U) plane and the half-filling singularity of f_{xc} manifest themselves in the energy/frequency dependence of χ(q,ω), and prove or disprove that these features persist once nonlocal and nonadiabatic corrections are included.
